/**
\page documentation Documentation

Peano and its toolboxes and extensions follow some very simplistic documentation paradigms:

- Everything is in the code;
- All documentation is created via doxygen;
- What does not fit into the code is documented in separated files which also are processed by doxygen;
- Code documentation should be in the header files for C/C++ code;
- Further documentation files have the extension .dox.

To generate the documentation, ```cd``` to your Peano directory, and type:

      doxygen documentation/Doxyfile
      

## Rationale

We aim for documentation which is always up-to-date.
The documentation shall be searchable.
We want to have one medium which integrates high-level overview information 
with source code documentation.


## How to add new files

Header files and Python scripts are automatically picked up by the script as 
long as they are stored within directories which are subject to search.
If you want to write stand-alone docu (such as high-level overview 
descriptions), the corresponding dox files should go into 

      documentation/Doxygen/SubDir

### Generic Pages

Doxygen supports the addition of pages anywhere in the directories that it searches. These can be written in 
markdown or html. 

Any page to be included should start with
      
```
\page page_ref Page Title 
```

Here, ```page_ref``` will serve as a global unique identifier throughout doxygen. 

Additionally, files must start with ```/**``` and end with ```* /```, so that Doxygen is alerted to process them.
So new pages, that are not code documentation, should look something like this:

```
/** 
\page page_ref Page Title

Content of my page, written in markdown language

* /

```


### Add new rubric to top-level menu

Top level menu entries are reserved for rubrics such as files, packages, ... or 
for our large-scale extensions such as ExaHyPE or Swift. Please consult with 
the team if you want to add a new top-level entry. 

If the team agrees that there should be a new top-level entry, you have to add
entries a new page somewhere within ```documentation/Doxygen```. Please note that any page marked with ``` page``` will appear on the top level menu. New documentation should therefore be structured as follows:

New Main Page
-------------

<ul>
<li> Begin the new main page as above (with a ``` page```, ```page_ref``` and title). </li>
<li> Enumerate all the subpages to be nested within this page below. This prevents these subpages from appearing at the top level menu. </li>
<li></li>
<li></li>

</ul>

New Sub Page
------------

Ensure that you follow normal rules for constructing a page (begin with ``` page```, include a reference and a title).

Example:
--------

In the new, top-level page:

```
 \page page_ref Page Title

Please see below a list of pages that are relevant here:

 \li \subpage subpage_ref

```

In the new subpage, where you go into more detail about the topics introduced in the new top-level page:

```
 page subpage_ref Subpage Title

Content coninues here...

```






### Add new page to ...




*/
